details.image_prompt:
[
  {
    "prompt": "Shot with a Canon EOS R5, 85mm f/1.8 lens. The composition features two simple, round, matte white ceramic dessert plates positioned on an aged, rustic oak wood table. The camera angle is 3/4 overhead, capturing the full arrangement of the dessert while maintaining a shallow depth of field where the front plate is tack-sharp and the second plate is softly blurred in the background. Lighting is soft natural window light from the left, casting gentle shadows and highlighting the warm, caramelized textures of the dish. Each plate holds two warm, broiled peach halves, placed side-by-side but not touching. Each peach half features a generous, rustic dollop of smooth, pearly-white vanilla-infused ricotta cream resting in the center well. A delicate drizzle of amber maple syrup pools slightly around the ricotta, with some drops catching the light. Toasted, golden-brown sliced almonds are scattered unevenly over the top, adding a textural crunch. The peach skins are a natural, vibrant orange-yellow with lightly charred, caramelized brown edges from the broiler. Props include a small, rumpled linen napkin off to one side and a few stray toasted almond slivers scattered naturally on the oak surface. Photorealistic, not AI-generated looking. The scene should look like someone just finished cooking and plating — lived-in, warm, authentic. Not a sterile studio setup. Slight natural imperfections: an asymmetric garnish placement, a small smear of maple syrup on the plate rim, slightly uneven browning on the fruit. Include subtle traces of the cooking process: a few toasted almond crumbs on the wooden table, a light dusting of cinnamon on the surface. No plastic sheen, no unnaturally smooth surfaces, no perfect symmetry. Warm, golden-hour color temperature — cozy and inviting. No text, no watermark, no logo, no hands, no faces. Indistinguishable from a professional cookbook photograph."
  }
]
